.\" Process this file with
.\" groff -man -Tascii mprotect.2
.\"
.TH MPROTECT 2 "July 2026" "Substrate Kernel" "System Calls Manual"
.SH NAME
mprotect \- set protection on a region of memory
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library (libc, -lc)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/mman.h>
.PP
.BI "int mprotect(void *" addr ", size_t " len ", int " prot ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mprotect()
changes the access protections for the calling process's memory pages
containing any part of the address range
.RI [ addr ", " addr + len ).
.PP
.I addr
must be page-aligned.
.I len
is rounded up to the next page boundary; a
.I len
of 0 is a no-op.
.PP
.I prot
is a bitwise OR of the following flags:
.TP
.B PROT_READ
The memory may be read.
.TP
.B PROT_WRITE
The memory may be written.
.TP
.B PROT_EXEC
The memory may be executed.
.PP
.B PROT_READ,
.B PROT_WRITE,
and
.B PROT_EXEC
have the values 0x1, 0x2, and 0x4, matching the kernel's internal
.B VM_PROT_*
constants.
The requested protection is applied to the underlying virtual-memory
map entries (not merely to already-faulted pages), so a page brought in
by a later demand fault is populated with the new protection.
.PP
The new protection is clamped to each mapping's maximum protection: a
region may only be reprotected to a subset of the protection it was
created with.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.B mprotect()
returns 0.
On error, \-1 is returned and
.I errno
is set to indicate the error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I addr
is not page-aligned, or the range
.RI [ addr ", " addr + len )
falls outside the user address space.
.TP
.B ENOMEM
The process has no address space, or the kernel could not apply the
protection change to the requested range.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mmap (2),
.BR munmap (2)
.SH NOTES
The Substrate dynamic linker
.RB ( /sbin/ld.so )
uses
.B mprotect()
to restore write-exclusive-or-execute (W^X) on shared-library text and
to make each object's
.B PT_GNU_RELRO
region read-only once relocations are applied.
